Split the frontend's ID3v2 tag setter into UTF-16 and UTF-8 variants.

Backported from LAME SVN trunk (svn.code.sf.net/p/lame/svn/trunk/lame):
frontend/parse.c, libmp3lame/id3tag.c, include/lame.h,
include/libmp3lame.sym, include/lame.def.

In 4.0 the single set_id3v2tag() declares its text argument as
'unsigned short const*', but id3_tag() hands it the result of toUtf8()
for TENC_UTF8, i.e. a UTF-8 char string. The TENC_UTF8 arm then passes
that pointer to id3tag_set_textinfo_utf8(), which takes 'char const*'
-- an incompatible-pointer-type error under GCC 14 -- and to the *_ucs2
setters, which really do want UTF-16 and would misparse the bytes.

This only bites where ID3TAGS_EXTENDED is defined, i.e. where configure
found a working iconv. Native linux-64 fails the iconv run test and so
never compiles the block; cross-compiled linux-ppc64le has to guess and
guesses yes, so it does.

Upstream fixed this by splitting the handler in two, each with the
correct pointer type, and by adding the missing UTF-8 counterpart
id3tag_set_fieldvalue_utf8() (ordinal @2031, matching upstream).

